    "rationale": "Earliest observed failure (R02) is on the very first action of every run: the incumbent builds a symmetric closure of `link` and its first emitted step is often a walk against a non-existent reverse link, so VAL rejects step 1 (all 10 tasks are plan_semantics with zero successes, and only 7 of the 21 planned actions ever execute). The task rules (R03) state link is directed and the reverse must not be assumed, so the fix is in the adjacency construction: drop the `adj[f[2]].add(f[1])` symmetric edge and use only directed link facts; if a nut or spanner is then unreachable, the planner emits the longest legal prefix (maximising tightened goal nuts) rather than illegal steps. Plan_length should fall (21/15 -> shorter legal prefixes) because redundant reverse traversals disappear; I claim plan_length is an identity-level statement of tightness, but the claim I bind is the directed-only structure replacing the symmetric assumption, which is exactly the change made here. Prediction is deliberately conservative: absolute increase of at least 1 success.",
